<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaybanids</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shaybanid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shaybanids were an Uzbek dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiban">Shiban</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, who ruled much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> in the sixteenth century. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-shaybani">Muhammad Shaybani Khan</a> they captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> between 1500 and 1507. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a> profile identifies these campaigns as the fatal blow that ended Timurid rule in Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p></div>
